A||N||10||2xsess||Recovering Seats (homemade)||||5||
Z||000000||2xsess||10-12-2000||10:17 PM||||I was thinking of making my own seat skins. My wife and I have recovered our couchs a couple of times. I've never had the covers off any of my Fiero seats. I was curious if anyone else has tried it. I figure if I do my own I can do it how I want it.<P><BR>Just a thought for now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ed||10-13-2000||12:02 AM||||I recovered mine with Mr. Mike's leather.<P>I originally went to an upholstery shop to get an estimate on a professional leather job.  He said that it was better if I could find a kit (which I did).  <P>That tells me that its a lot less grief to use Mr. Mike's leather or the Fiero Shop's cloth than to try a "do it yourself." ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||10-13-2000||08:14 AM||||2xsess,<P>If you are capable of making something like this go ahead.  There really aren't any good alternatives out there for us fans of cloth.  I do realize that the Fiero Store carries some, but at $400 for the set... that is much too steep to be feasible.  <P>If you guys get good at this, you could certainly make a bundle.  Keep us all posted.  Almost every Fiero owner that I know either wants to replace their seat-backs or wants an extra for when they do finally rip out!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000003||2xsess||10-13-2000||08:30 AM||||I'm going to take the cover off my drivers side this weekend and look at it to determine if I can do it and have professional looking results. <P>I agree, $400 for cloth is way too much.<P>Anyone know where I can get hogring pliers and rings? Will the local auto parts place carry these?<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<P><p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||10-13-2000||09:42 AM||||My wife has agreed to make a set for me.  I removed the driver's side covers a couple weeks ago so she can look at them.  Spent quite awhile searching the web for cloth.  Then, found some locally that I like better for half the price.  <P>She hasn't gotten started yet -- for one thing, I haven't bought the cloth and for another, she is painting the house!!  I figure it will be a good winter project for her when there is no grass to mow and the house is painted, etc.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fret Naught||10-13-2000||11:36 AM||||Your wife cuts the grass paints the house and does upolstery!!!!! DUDE you better hang on to HER!!!!!||||reg||10
Z||000006||2xsess||10-13-2000||12:14 PM||||Man, sounds like you have the dream wife. lol<P>It doesn't seem to be any special type of cloth. I'm going to go look tonight. <P>Any ideas on how to make the speaker holes?<BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||10-13-2000||01:19 PM||||She sounds like the wife in the commercial that comes home after shopping with a chainsaw, cordless drill and beer.  Does she have a sister?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000008||mbramble||10-13-2000||01:28 PM||||No, she has 6 brothers!!!<P>You should be able to find a local automotive upholstery shop that will sell you the right material.  The one here has some that is almost identical to what is in my car now.  It's $15/yd.  And they have the exact color vinyl for the lower portions that are vinyl.  They also have a really nice looking medium grey (same color as my original) material with some maroon threads in the background that ought to match really nice with the exterior color.  The lady told me to just bring in the car and they will lay some fabric over the seats to see what looks the best.  <p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||10-13-2000||02:23 PM||||Hogrings and the tool are available at Home Depot. I've seen them there. I was going to buy them until I found out Mr. Mike's kit includes 2 tools and a bag of hogrings.<BR>I would send you the tools and extra rings but I gave them to DXR_DAD when I sold him my original seat skins.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2xsess||10-13-2000||03:36 PM||||Damn, if only my local Home Depot wasn't an hour away.lol. <P>Does the fabric have a different backing or what? How many yards do you think it would take to do two seats? I was thinking of using the original vinyl from the lower and back parts of the seat.<P>Ok, I just had my passenger seat out of the car and I looked it over. I need to get the rings and pliers before I even begin.<P>I also looked closely at my drivers seat, there is a chunk of the foam missing at the bolster area. I guess I could probably stuff another piece in there. And the vinyl isn't going to be able to be reused. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I'm still going to try it once I have the proper tools on hand, I'll keep you all posted. I'll probably try Wal-mart tonight. If not then I guess I'll be making the hour drive to Home Depot tomorrow.<P>Thanks for all the advice.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
